Yes, the surgical robotics simulator does have sensors
Based on my exploration of the codebase, here's what sensors are available in the AMBF Surgical Robotics Challenge simulator:

Built-in Sensors:
Joint Position & Velocity Sensors (all 6 PSM joints)

measured_jp() — reads joint positions for the 6 main degrees of freedom
measured_jv() — reads joint velocities
Accessible through the PSM arm class (psm_arm.py)
End-Effector Pose Sensors

measured_cp() — measures tool tip position and orientation (Cartesian pose)
Forward kinematics computed from joint positions
Jaw/Gripper Angle Sensor

get_jaw_angle() — reads the gripper opening angle
Used for grasping tasks
Contact Sensors

The simulator was updated (v2.0.0, 2024) to implement "a grasping algorithm using contact sensors"
These are used for grasp detection and manipulation
Camera System (Stereo Vision)

Dual camera setup (left/right) defined in world_stereo.yaml
640×480 RGB image resolution
Depth camera support (also 640×480)
Can publish scene images and depth data (PointCloud2)
Camera feeds are available through ROS topics
State Available Through API:
Robot joint/Cartesian state
Needle position and orientation
Task completion metrics
Simulation time and synchronization
These sensors expose the fundamental data needed for control, learning, and evaluation of surgical manipulation tasks like needle grasping and suturing.



- gripper 
Each gripper (left and right fingers) has an invisible ghost body that acts as a contact sensor:
Key Points
✅ Contact-based detection — no explicit force sensor, but collision detection
✅ Both finger sensors — left and right fingers report sensed objects independently
✅ Automatic grasp logic — built-in run_grasp_logic() handles the detection/grasping automatically
✅ Graspable objects — can detect Needle, Thread, Puzzle pieces, and blocks
